<b></b><p align=3D"CENTER"><b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b> </p>
<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:
</b>
     Our gain in net worth during 1986 was $492.5 million, or=20
26.1%.  Over the last 22 years (that is, since present management=20
took over), our per-share book value has grown from $19.46 to=20
$2,073.06, or 23.3% compounded annually.  Both the numerator and=20
denominator are important in the per-share book value=20
calculation: during the 22-year period our corporate net worth=20
has increased 10,600% while shares outstanding have increased=20
less than 1%.

     In past reports I have noted that book value at most=20
companies differs widely from intrinsic business value - the=20
number that really counts for owners.  In our own case, however,=20
book value has served for more than a decade as a reasonable if=20
somewhat conservative proxy for business value.  That is, our=20
business value has moderately exceeded our book value, with the=20
ratio between the two remaining fairly steady.

     The good news is that in 1986 our percentage gain in=20
business value probably exceeded the book value gain.  I say=20
"probably" because business value is a soft number: in our own=20
case, two equally well-informed observers might make judgments=20
more than 10% apart.

     A large measure of our improvement in business value=20
relative to book value reflects the outstanding performance of=20
key managers at our major operating businesses.  These managers -=20
the Blumkins, Mike Goldberg, the Heldmans, Chuck Huggins, Stan=20
Lipsey, and Ralph Schey - have over the years improved the=20
earnings of their businesses dramatically while, except in the=20
case of insurance, utilizing little additional capital.  This=20
accomplishment builds economic value, or "Goodwill," that does=20
not show up in the net worth figure on our balance sheet, nor in=20
our per-share book value.  In 1986 this unrecorded gain was=20
substantial.

     So much for the good news.  The bad news is that my=20
performance did not match that of our managers.  While they were=20
doing a superb job in running our businesses, I was unable to=20
skillfully deploy much of the capital they generated.
=20
     Charlie Munger, our Vice Chairman, and I really have only=20
two jobs.  One is to attract and keep outstanding managers to run=20
our various operations.  This hasn=92t been all that difficult. =20
Usually the managers came with the companies we bought, having=20
demonstrated their talents throughout careers that spanned a wide=20
variety of business circumstances.  They were managerial stars=20
long before they knew us, and our main contribution has been to=20
not get in their way.  This approach seems elementary: if my job=20
were to manage a golf team - and if Jack Nicklaus or Arnold=20
Palmer were willing to play for me - neither would get a lot of=20
directives from me about how to swing.

     Some of our key managers are independently wealthy (we hope=20
they all become so), but that poses no threat to their continued=20
interest: they work because they love what they do and relish the=20
thrill of outstanding performance.  They unfailingly think like=20
owners (the highest compliment we can pay a manager) and find all=20
aspects of their business absorbing.

     (Our prototype for occupational fervor is the Catholic=20
tailor who used his small savings of many years to finance a=20
pilgrimage to the Vatican.  When he returned, his parish held a=20
special meeting to get his first-hand account of the Pope.  "Tell=20
us," said the eager faithful, "just what sort of fellow is he?"=20
Our hero wasted no words: "He=92s a forty-four, medium.")

     Charlie and I know that the right players will make almost=20
any team manager look good.  We subscribe to the philosophy of=20
Ogilvy &amp; Mather=92s founding genius, David Ogilvy: "If each of us=20
hires people who are smaller than we are, we shall become a=20
company of dwarfs.  But, if each of us hires people who are=20
bigger than we are, we shall become a company of giants."

     A by-product of our managerial style is the ability it gives=20
us to easily expand Berkshire=92s activities.  We=92ve read=20
management treatises that specify exactly how many people should=20
report to any one executive, but they make little sense to us. =20
When you have able managers of high character running businesses=20
about which they are passionate, you can have a dozen or more=20
reporting to you and still have time for an afternoon nap. =20
Conversely, if you have even one person reporting to you who is=20
deceitful, inept or uninterested, you will find yourself with=20
more than you can handle.  Charlie and I could work with double=20
the number of managers we now have, so long as they had the rare=20
qualities of the present ones.

     We intend to continue our practice of working only with=20
people whom we like and admire.  This policy not only maximizes=20
our chances for good results, it also ensures us an=20
extraordinarily good time.  On the other hand, working with=20
people who cause your stomach to churn seems much like marrying=20
for money - probably a bad idea under any circumstances, but=20
absolute madness if you are already rich.

     The second job Charlie and I must handle is the allocation=20
of capital, which at Berkshire is a considerably more important=20
challenge than at most companies.  Three factors make that so: we=20
earn more money than average; we retain all that we earn; and, we=20
are fortunate to have operations that, for the most part, require=20
little incremental capital to remain competitive and to grow. =20
Obviously, the future results of a business earning 23% annually=20
and retaining it all are far more affected by today=92s capital=20
allocations than are the results of a business earning 10% and=20
distributing half of that to shareholders.  If our retained=20
earnings - and those of our major investees, GEICO and Capital=20
Cities/ABC, Inc. - are employed in an unproductive manner, the=20
economics of Berkshire will deteriorate very quickly.  In a=20
company adding only, say, 5% to net worth annually, capital-
allocation decisions, though still important, will change the=20
company=92s economics far more slowly.

     Capital allocation at Berkshire was tough work in 1986.  We=20
did make one business acquisition - The Fechheimer Bros. =20
Company, which we will discuss in a later section.  Fechheimer is=20
a company with excellent economics, run by exactly the kind of=20
people with whom we enjoy being associated.  But it is relatively=20
small, utilizing only about 2% of Berkshire=92s net worth.

     Meanwhile, we had no new ideas in the marketable equities=20
field, an area in which once, only a few years ago, we could=20
readily employ large sums in outstanding businesses at very=20
reasonable prices.  So our main capital allocation moves in 1986=20
were to pay off debt and stockpile funds.  Neither is a fate=20
worse than death, but they do not inspire us to do handsprings=20
either.  If Charlie and I were to draw blanks for a few years in=20
our capital-allocation endeavors, Berkshire=92s rate of growth=20
would slow significantly.

     We will continue to look for operating businesses that meet=20
our tests and, with luck, will acquire such a business every=20
couple of years.  But an acquisition will have to be large if it=20
is to help our performance materially.  Under current stock=20
market conditions, we have little hope of finding equities to buy=20
for our insurance companies.  Markets will change significantly -=20
you can be sure of that and some day we will again get our turn=20
at bat.  However, we haven=92t the faintest idea when that might=20
happen.

     It can=92t be said too often (although I=92m sure you feel I=92ve=20
tried) that, even under favorable conditions, our returns are=20
certain to drop substantially because of our enlarged size.  We=20
have told you that we hope to average a return of 15% on equity=20
and we maintain that hope, despite some negative tax law changes=20
described in a later section of this report.  If we are to=20
achieve this rate of return, our net worth must increase $7.2=20
billion in the next ten years.  A gain of that magnitude will be=20
possible only if, before too long, we come up with a few very big=20
(and good) ideas.  Charlie and I can=92t promise results, but we do=20
promise you that we will keep our efforts focused on our goals.

<b>Sources of Reported Earnings
</b>
     The table on the next page shows the major sources of=20
Berkshire=92s reported earnings.  This table differs in several=20
ways from the one presented last year.  We have added four new=20
lines of business because of the Scott Fetzer and Fechheimer=20
acquisitions. In the case of Scott Fetzer, the two major units=20
acquired were World Book and Kirby, and each is presented=20
separately.  Fourteen other businesses of Scott Fetzer are=20
aggregated in Scott Fetzer - Diversified Manufacturing.  SF=20
Financial Group, a credit company holding both World Book and=20
Kirby receivables, is included in "Other." This year, because=20
Berkshire is so much larger, we also have eliminated separate=20
reporting for several of our smaller businesses.

     In the table, amortization of Goodwill is not charged=20
against the specific businesses but, for reasons outlined in the=20
Appendix to my letter in the 1983 Annual Report, is aggregated as=20
a separate item. (A Compendium of earlier letters, including the=20
Goodwill discussion, is available upon request.) Both the Scott=20
Fetzer and Fechheimer acquisitions created accounting Goodwill,=20
which is why the amortization charge for Goodwill increased in=20
1986.

     Additionally, the Scott Fetzer acquisition required other=20
major purchase-price accounting adjustments, as prescribed by=20
generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP).  The GAAP=20
figures, of course, are the ones used in our consolidated=20
financial statements.  But, in our view, the GAAP figures are not=20
necessarily the most useful ones for investors or managers. =20
Therefore, the figures shown for specific operating units are=20
earnings before purchase-price adjustments are taken into=20
account.  In effect, these are the earnings that would have been=20
reported by the businesses if we had not purchased them.

     A discussion of our reasons for preferring this form of=20
presentation is in the Appendix to this letter.  This Appendix=20
will never substitute for a steamy novel and definitely is not=20
required reading.  However, I know that among our 6,000=20
shareholders there are those who are thrilled by my essays on=20
accounting - and I hope that both of you enjoy the Appendix.

     In the Business Segment Data on pages 41-43 and in the=20
Management=92s Discussion section on pages 45-49, you will find=20
much additional information about our businesses.  I urge you to=20
read those sections, as well as Charlie Munger=92s letter to Wesco=20
shareholders, describing the various businesses of that=20
subsidiary, which starts on page 50.
