     As you can see, operating earnings substantially improved=20
during 1986.  Some of the improvement came from the insurance=20
operation, whose results I will discuss in a later section. =20
Fechheimer also will be discussed separately.  Our other major=20
businesses performed as follows:

   <b>o</b> Operating results at The Buffalo News continue to reflect a=20
truly superb managerial job by Stan Lipsey.  For the third year=20
in a row, man-hours worked fell significantly and other costs=20
were closely controlled.  Consequently, our operating margins=20
improved materially in 1986, even though our advertising rate=20
increases were well below those of most major newspapers.

     Our cost-control efforts have in no way reduced our=20
commitment to news.  We continue to deliver a 50% "news hole"=20
(the portion of the total space in the paper devoted to news), a=20
higher percentage, we believe, than exists at any dominant=20
newspaper in this country of our size or larger.

     The average news hole at papers comparable to the News is=20
about 40%.  The difference between 40% and 50% is more important=20
than it might first seem: a paper with 30 pages of ads and a 40%=20
news hole delivers 20 pages of news a day, whereas our paper=20
matches 30 pages of ads with 30 pages of news.  Therefore, given=20
ad pages equal in number, we end up delivering our readers no=20
less than 50% more news.

     We believe this heavy commitment to news is one of the=20
reasons The Buffalo News has the highest weekday penetration rate=20
(the percentage of households in the paper=92s primary marketing=20
area purchasing it each day) among any of the top 50 papers in=20
the country.  Our Sunday penetration, where we are also number=20
one, is even more impressive.  Ten years ago, the only Sunday=20
paper serving Buffalo (the Courier-Express) had circulation of=20
271,000 and a penetration ratio of about 63%.  The Courier-
Express had served the area for many decades and its penetration=20
ratio - which was similar to those existing in many metropolitan=20
markets - was thought to be a "natural" one, accurately=20
reflecting the local citizenry=92s appetite for a Sunday product.

     Our Sunday paper was started in late 1977.  It now has a=20
penetration ratio of 83% and sells about 100,000 copies more each=20
Sunday than did the Courier-Express ten years ago - even though=20
population in our market area has declined during the decade.  In=20
recent history, no other city that has long had a local Sunday=20
paper has experienced a penetration gain anywhere close to=20
Buffalo=92s.

     Despite our exceptional market acceptance, our operating=20
margins almost certainly have peaked.  A major newsprint price=20
increase took effect at the end of 1986, and our advertising rate=20
increases in 1987 will again be moderate compared to those of the=20
industry.  However, even if margins should materially shrink, we=20
would not reduce our news-hole ratio.

     As I write this, it has been exactly ten years since we=20
purchased The News.  The financial rewards it has brought us have=20
far exceeded our expectations and so, too, have the non-financial=20
rewards.  Our respect for the News - high when we bought it - has=20
grown consistently ever since the purchase, as has our respect=20
and admiration for Murray Light, the editor who turns out the=20
product that receives such extraordinary community acceptance. =20
The efforts of Murray and Stan, which were crucial to the News=20
during its dark days of financial reversals and litigation, have=20
not in the least been lessened by prosperity.  Charlie and I are=20
grateful to them.

   <b>o</b> The amazing Blumkins continue to perform business miracles=20
at Nebraska Furniture Mart.  Competitors come and go (mostly go),=20
but Mrs. B. and her progeny roll on.  In 1986 net sales increased=20
10.2% to $132 million.  Ten years ago sales were $44 million and,=20
even then, NFM appeared to be doing just about all of the=20
business available in the Greater Omaha Area.  Given NFM=92s=20
remarkable dominance, Omaha=92s slow growth in population and the=20
modest inflation rates that have applied to the goods NFM sells,=20
how can this operation continue to rack up such large sales=20
gains?  The only logical explanation is that the marketing=20
territory of NFM=92s one-and-only store continues to widen because=20
of its ever-growing reputation for rock-bottom everyday prices=20
and the broadest of selections.  In preparation for further=20
gains, NFM is expanding the capacity of its warehouse, located a=20
few hundred yards from the store, by about one-third.

     Mrs. B, Chairman of Nebraska Furniture Mart, continues at=20
age 93 to outsell and out-hustle any manager I=92ve ever seen. =20
She=92s at the store seven days a week, from opening to close. =20
Competing with her represents a triumph of courage over judgment.

     It=92s easy to overlook what I consider to be the critical=20
lesson of the Mrs. B saga: at 93, Omaha based Board Chairmen have=20
yet to reach their peak.  Please file this fact away to consult=20
before you mark your ballot at the 2024 annual meeting of=20
Berkshire.

   <b>o</b> At See=92s, sales trends improved somewhat from those of=20
recent years.  Total pounds sold rose about 2%. (For you=20
chocaholics who like to fantasize, one statistic: we sell over=20
12,000 tons annually.) Same-store sales, measured in pounds, were=20
virtually unchanged.  In the previous six years, same store=20
poundage fell, and we gained or maintained poundage volume only=20
by adding stores.  But a particularly strong Christmas season in=20
1986 stemmed the decline.  By stabilizing same-store volume and=20
making a major effort to control costs, See=92s was able to=20
maintain its excellent profit margin in 1986 though it put=20
through only minimal price increases.  We have Chuck Huggins, our=20
long-time manager at See=92s, to thank for this significant=20
achievement.

     See=92s has a one-of-a-kind product "personality" produced by=20
a combination of its candy=92s delicious taste and moderate price,=20
the company=92s total control of the distribution process, and the=20
exceptional service provided by store employees.  Chuck=20
rightfully measures his success by the satisfaction of our=20
customers, and his attitude permeates the organization.  Few=20
major retailing companies have been able to sustain such a=20
customer-oriented spirit, and we owe Chuck a great deal for=20
keeping it alive and well at See=92s.

     See=92s profits should stay at about their present level.  We=20
will continue to increase prices very modestly, merely matching=20
prospective cost increases.

   <b>o</b> World Book is the largest of 17 Scott Fetzer operations=20
that joined Berkshire at the beginning of 1986.  Last year I=20
reported to you enthusiastically about the businesses of Scott=20
Fetzer and about Ralph Schey, its manager.  A year=92s experience=20
has added to my enthusiasm for both.  Ralph is a superb=20
businessman and a straight shooter.  He also brings exceptional=20
versatility and energy to his job: despite the wide array of=20
businesses that he manages, he is on top of the operations,=20
opportunities and problems of each.  And, like our other=20
managers, Ralph is a real pleasure to work with.  Our good=20
fortune continues.

     World Book=92s unit volume increased for the fourth=20
consecutive year, with encyclopedia sales up 7% over 1985 and 45%=20
over 1982.  Childcraft=92s unit sales also grew significantly.

     World Book continues to dominate the U.S. direct-sales=20
encyclopedia market - and for good reasons.  Extraordinarily=20
well-edited and priced at under 5 cents per page, these books are=20
a bargain for youngster and adult alike.  You may find one=20
editing technique interesting: World Book ranks over 44,000 words=20
by difficulty.  Longer entries in the encyclopedia include only=20
the most easily comprehended words in the opening sections, with=20
the difficulty of the material gradually escalating as the=20
exposition proceeds.  As a result, youngsters can easily and=20
profitably read to the point at which subject matter gets too=20
difficult, instead of immediately having to deal with a=20
discussion that mixes up words requiring college-level=20
comprehension with others of fourth-grade level. =20

     Selling World Book is a calling.  Over one-half of our=20
active salespeople are teachers or former teachers, and another=20
5% have had experience as librarians.  They correctly think of=20
themselves as educators, and they do a terrific job.  If you=20
don=92t have a World Book set in your house, I recommend one.

   <b>o</b> Kirby likewise recorded its fourth straight year of unit=20
volume gains.  Worldwide, unit sales grew 4% from 1985 and 33%=20
from 1982.  While the Kirby product is more expensive than most=20
cleaners, it performs in a manner that leaves cheaper units far=20
behind ("in the dust," so to speak).  Many 30- and 40-year-old=20
Kirby cleaners are still in active duty.  If you want the best,=20
you buy a Kirby.

     Some companies that historically have had great success in=20
direct sales have stumbled in recent years.  Certainly the era of=20
the working woman has created new challenges for direct sales=20
organizations.  So far, the record shows that both Kirby and=20
World Book have responded most successfully.

     The businesses described above, along with the insurance=20
operation and Fechheimer, constitute our major business units. =20
The brevity of our descriptions is in no way meant to diminish=20
the importance of these businesses to us.  All have been=20
discussed in past annual reports and, because of the tendency of=20
Berkshire owners to stay in the fold (about 98% of the stock at=20
the end of each year is owned by people who were owners at the=20
start of the year), we want to avoid undue repetition of basic=20
facts.  You can be sure that we will immediately report to you in=20
detail if the underlying economics or competitive position of any=20
of these businesses should materially change.  In general, the=20
businesses described in this section can be characterized as=20
having very strong market positions, very high returns on capital=20
employed, and the best of operating managements.

<b>The Fechheimer Bros. Co.
</b>
     Every year in Berkshire=92s annual report I include a=20
description of the kind of business that we would like to buy. =20
This "ad" paid off in 1986.

     On January 15th of last year I received a letter from Bob=20
Heldman of Cincinnati, a shareholder for many years and also=20
Chairman of Fechheimer Bros.  Until I read the letter, however, I=20
did not know of either Bob or Fechheimer.  Bob wrote that he ran=20
a company that met our tests and suggested that we get together,=20
which we did in Omaha after their results for 1985 were compiled.

     He filled me in on a little history: Fechheimer, a uniform=20
manufacturing and distribution business, began operations in=20
1842.  Warren Heldman, Bob=92s father, became involved in the=20
business in 1941 and his sons, Bob and George (now President),=20
along with their sons, subsequently joined the company.  Under=20
the Heldmans=92 management, the business was highly successful.

     In 1981 Fechheimer was sold to a group of venture=20
capitalists in a leveraged buy out (an LBO), with management=20
retaining an equity interest.  The new company, as is the case=20
with all LBOS, started with an exceptionally high debt/equity=20
ratio.  After the buy out, however, operations continued to be=20
very successful.  So by the start of last year debt had been paid=20
down substantially and the value of the equity had increased=20
dramatically.  For a variety of reasons, the venture capitalists=20
wished to sell and Bob, having dutifully read Berkshire=92s annual=20
reports, thought of us.

     Fechheimer is exactly the sort of business we like to buy. =20
Its economic record is superb; its managers are talented, high-
grade, and love what they do; and the Heldman family wanted to=20
continue its financial interest in partnership with us. =20
Therefore, we quickly purchased about 84% of the stock for a=20
price that was based upon a $55 million valuation for the entire=20
business.

     The circumstances of this acquisition were similar to those=20
prevailing in our purchase of Nebraska Furniture Mart: most of=20
the shares were held by people who wished to employ funds=20
elsewhere; family members who enjoyed running their business=20
wanted to continue both as owners and managers; several=20
generations of the family were active in the business, providing=20
management for as far as the eye can see; and the managing family=20
wanted a purchaser who would not re-sell, regardless of price,=20
and who would let the business be run in the future as it had=20
been in the past.  Both Fechheimer and NFM were right for us, and=20
we were right for them.

     You may be amused to know that neither Charlie nor I have=20
been to Cincinnati, headquarters for Fechheimer, to see their=20
operation. (And, incidentally, it works both ways: Chuck Huggins,=20
who has been running See=92s for 15 years, has never been to=20
Omaha.) If our success were to depend upon insights we developed=20
through plant inspections, Berkshire would be in big trouble. =20
Rather, in considering an acquisition, we attempt to evaluate the=20
economic characteristics of the business - its competitive=20
strengths and weaknesses - and the quality of the people we will=20
be joining.  Fechheimer was a standout in both respects.  In=20
addition to Bob and George Heldman, who are in their mid-60s -=20
spring chickens by our standards - there are three members of the=20
next generation, Gary, Roger and Fred, to insure continuity.

     As a prototype for acquisitions, Fechheimer has only one=20
drawback: size.  We hope our next acquisition is at least several=20
times as large but a carbon copy in all other respects.  Our=20
threshold for minimum annual after-tax earnings of potential=20
acquisitions has been moved up to $10 million from the $5 million=20
level that prevailed when Bob wrote to me.

     Flushed with success, we repeat our ad.  If you have a=20
business that fits, call me or, preferably, write.

     Here=92s what we=92re looking for:=20
     (1) large purchases (at least $10 million of after-tax=20
         earnings),=20
     (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
         projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
         "turn-around" situations),=20
     (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
         employing little or no debt. =20
     (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),=20
     (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we=20
         won=92t understand it),=20
     (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time=20
         or that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
         about a transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we=92re interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuing stock when we receive=20
as much in intrinsic business value as we give.  Indeed,=20
following recent advances in the price of Berkshire stock,=20
transactions involving stock issuance may be quite feasible.  We=20
invite potential sellers to check us out by contacting people=20
with whom we have done business in the past.  For the right=20
business - and the right people - we can provide a good home. =20

     On the other hand, we frequently get approached about=20
acquisitions that don=92t come close to meeting our tests: new=20
ventures, turnarounds, auction-like sales, and the ever-popular=20
(among brokers) "I=92m-sure-something-will-work-out-if-you-people-
get-to-know-each-other." None of these attracts us in the least.

                         *  *  *

     Besides being interested in the purchases of entire=20
businesses as described above, we are also interested in the=20
negotiated purchase of large, but not controlling, blocks of=20
stock, as in our Cap Cities purchase.  Such purchases appeal to=20
us only when we are very comfortable with both the economics of=20
the business and the ability and integrity of the people running=20
the operation.  We prefer large transactions: in the unusual case=20
we might do something as small as $50 million (or even smaller),=20
but our preference is for commitments many times that size.

