     The combined ratio represents total insurance costs (losses=20
incurred plus expenses) compared to revenue from premiums: a=20
ratio below 100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above=20
100 indicates a loss.  When the investment income that an insurer=20
earns from holding on to policyholders=92 funds ("the float") is=20
taken into account, a combined ratio in the 107-112 range=20
typically produces an overall break-even result, exclusive of=20
earnings on the funds provided by shareholders.

     The math of the insurance business, encapsulated by the=20
table, is not very complicated.  In years when the industry=92s=20
annual gain in revenues (premiums) pokes along at 4% or 5%,=20
underwriting losses are sure to mount.  This is not because auto=20
accidents, fires, windstorms and the like are occurring more=20
frequently, nor has it lately been the fault of general=20
inflation.  Today, social and judicial inflation are the major=20
culprits; the cost of entering a courtroom has simply ballooned. =20
Part of the jump in cost arises from skyrocketing verdicts, and=20
part from the tendency of judges and juries to expand the=20
coverage of insurance policies beyond that contemplated by the=20
insurer when the policies were written.  Seeing no let-up in=20
either trend, we continue to believe that the industry=92s revenues=20
must grow at close to 10% annually for it to just hold its own=20
in terms of profitability, even though general inflation may be=20
running only 2% - 4%.

     In 1986, as noted, the industry=92s premium volume soared even=20
faster than loss costs.  Consequently, the underwriting loss of=20
the industry fell dramatically.  In last year=92s report we=20
predicted this sharp improvement but also predicted that=20
prosperity would be fleeting.  Alas, this second prediction is=20
already proving accurate.  The rate of gain in the industry=92s=20
premium volume has slowed significantly (from an estimated 27.1%=20
in 1986=92s first quarter, to 23.5% in the second, to 21.8% in the=20
third, to 18.7% in the fourth), and we expect further slowing in=20
1987.  Indeed, the rate of gain may well fall below my 10%=20
"equilibrium" figure by the third quarter.

     Nevertheless, underwriting results in 1987, assuming they=20
are not dragged down by a major natural catastrophe, will again=20
improve materially because price increases are recognized in=20
revenues on a lagged basis.  In effect, the good news in earnings=20
follows the good news in prices by six to twelve months.  But the=20
improving trend in earnings will probably end by late 1988 or=20
early 1989.  Thereafter the industry is likely to head south in a=20
hurry.

     Pricing behavior in the insurance industry continues to be=20
exactly what can be expected in a commodity-type business.  Only=20
under shortage conditions are high profits achieved, and such=20
conditions don=92t last long.  When the profit sun begins to shine,=20
long-established insurers shower investors with new shares in=20
order to build capital.  In addition, newly-formed insurers rush=20
to sell shares at the advantageous prices available in the new-
issue market (prices advantageous, that is, to the insiders=20
promoting the company but rarely to the new shareholders).  These=20
moves guarantee future trouble: capacity soars, competitive=20
juices flow, and prices fade.

     It=92s interesting to observe insurance leaders beseech their=20
colleagues to behave in a more "statesmanlike" manner when=20
pricing policies.  "Why," they ask, "can=92t we learn from history,=20
even out the peaks and valleys, and consistently price to make=20
reasonable profits?" What they wish, of course, is pricing that=20
resembles, say, that of The Wall Street journal, whose prices are=20
ample to start with and rise consistently each year.

     Such calls for improved behavior have all of the efficacy of=20
those made by a Nebraska corn grower asking his fellow growers,=20
worldwide, to market their corn with more statesmanship.  What=92s=20
needed is not more statesmen, but less corn.  By raising large=20
amounts of capital in the last two years, the insurance industry=20
has, to continue our metaphor, vastly expanded its plantings of=20
corn.  The resulting increase in "crop" - i.e., the proliferation=20
of insurance capacity - will have the same effect on prices and=20
profits that surplus crops have had since time immemorial.

     Our own insurance operation did well in 1986 and is also=20
likely to do well in 1987.  We have benefited significantly from=20
industry conditions.  But much of our prosperity arises from the=20
efforts and ability of Mike Goldberg, manager of all insurance=20
operations.

     Our combined ratio (on a statutory basis and excluding=20
structured settlements and financial reinsurance) fell from 111=20
in 1985 to 103 in 1986.  In addition, our premium growth has been=20
exceptional: although final figures aren=92t available, I believe=20
that over the past two years we were the fastest growing company=20
among the country=92s top 100 insurers.  Some of our growth, it is=20
true, came from our large quota-share contract with Fireman=92s=20
Fund, described in last year=92s report and updated in Charlie=92s=20
letter on page 54.  But even if the premiums from that contract=20
are excluded from the calculation, we probably still ranked first=20
in growth.

     Interestingly, we were the slowest-growing large insurer in=20
the years immediately preceding 1985.  In fact, we shrank - and=20
we will do so again from time to time in the future.  Our large=20
swings in volume do not mean that we come and go from the=20
insurance marketplace.  Indeed, we are its most steadfast=20
participant, always standing ready, at prices we believe=20
adequate, to write a wide variety of high-limit coverages.  The=20
swings in our volume arise instead from the here-today, gone-
tomorrow behavior of other insurers.  When most insurers are=20
"gone," because their capital is inadequate or they have been=20
frightened by losses, insureds rush to us and find us ready to=20
do business.  But when hordes of insurers are "here," and are=20
slashing prices far below expectable costs, many customers=20
naturally leave us in order to take advantage of the bargains=20
temporarily being offered by our competition.

     Our firmness on prices works no hardship on the consumer: he=20
is being bombarded by attractively priced insurance offers at=20
those times when we are doing little business.  And it works no=20
hardship on our employees: we don=92t engage in layoffs when we=20
experience a cyclical slowdown at one of our generally-profitable=20
insurance operations.  This no-layoff practice is in our self-
interest.  Employees who fear that large layoffs will accompany=20
sizable reductions in premium volume will understandably produce=20
scads of business through thick and thin (mostly thin).

     The trends in National Indemnity=92s traditional business -=20
the writing of commercial auto and general liability policies=20
through general agents - suggest how gun-shy other insurers=20
became for a while and how brave they are now getting.  In the=20
last quarter of 1984, NICO=92s monthly volume averaged $5 million,=20
about what it had been running for several years.  By the first=20
quarter of 1986, monthly volume had climbed to about $35 million. =20
In recent months, a sharp decline has set in.  Monthly volume is=20
currently about $20 million and will continue to fall as new=20
competitors surface and prices are cut.  Ironically, the managers=20
of certain major new competitors are the very same managers that=20
just a few years ago bankrupted insurers that were our old=20
competitors.  Through state-mandated guaranty funds, we must pay=20
some of the losses these managers left unpaid, and now we find=20
them writing the same sort of business under a new name.  C=92est=20
la guerre.

     The business we call "large risks" expanded significantly=20
during 1986, and will be important to us in the future.  In this=20
operation, we regularly write policies with annual premiums of $1=20
- $3 million, or even higher.  This business will necessarily be=20
highly volatile - both in volume and profitability - but our=20
premier capital position and willingness to write large net lines=20
make us a very strong force in the market when prices are right. =20
On the other hand, our structured settlement business has become=20
near-dormant because present prices make no sense to us.

     The 1986 loss reserve development of our insurance group is=20
chronicled on page 46.  The figures show the amount of error in=20
our yearend 1985 liabilities that a year of settlements and=20
further evaluation has revealed.  As you can see, what I told you=20
last year about our loss liabilities was far from true - and that=20
makes three years in a row of error.  If the physiological rules=20
that applied to Pinocchio were to apply to me, my nose would now=20
draw crowds.

     When insurance executives belatedly establish proper=20
reserves, they often speak of "reserve strengthening," a term=20
that has a rather noble ring to it.  They almost make it sound as=20
if they are adding extra layers of strength to an already-solid=20
balance sheet.  That=92s not the case: instead the term is a=20
euphemism for what should more properly be called "correction of=20
previous untruths" (albeit non-intentional ones).

     We made a special effort at the end of 1986 to reserve=20
accurately.  However, we tried just as hard at the end of 1985. =20
Only time will tell whether we have finally succeeded in=20
correctly estimating our insurance liabilities.

     Despite the difficulties we have had in reserving and the=20
commodity economics of the industry, we expect our insurance=20
business to both grow and make significant amounts of money - but=20
progress will be distinctly irregular and there will be major=20
unpleasant surprises from time to time.  It=92s a treacherous=20
business and a wary attitude is essential.  We must heed Woody=20
Allen: "While the lamb may lie down with the lion, the lamb=20
shouldn=92t count on getting a whole lot of sleep."

     In our insurance operations we have an advantage in=20
attitude, we have an advantage in capital, and we are developing=20
an advantage in personnel.  Additionally, I like to think we have=20
some long-term edge in investing the float developed from=20
policyholder funds.  The nature of the business suggests that we=20
will need all of these advantages in order to prosper.

