<b>Marketable Securities
</b>
     During 1986, our insurance companies purchased about $700=20
million of tax-exempt bonds, most having a maturity of 8 to 12=20
years.  You might think that this commitment indicates a=20
considerable enthusiasm for such bonds.  Unfortunately, that=92s=20
not so: at best, the bonds are mediocre investments.  They simply=20
seemed the least objectionable alternative at the time we bought=20
them, and still seem so. (Currently liking neither stocks nor=20
bonds, I find myself the polar opposite of Mae West as she=20
declared: "I like only two kinds of men - foreign and domestic.")

     We must, of necessity, hold marketable securities in our=20
insurance companies and, as money comes in, we have only five=20
directions to go: (1) long-term common stock investments; (2)=20
long-term fixed-income securities; (3) medium-term fixed-income=20
securities; (4) short-term cash equivalents; and (5) short-term=20
arbitrage commitments.

     Common stocks, of course, are the most fun.  When conditions=20
are right that is, when companies with good economics and good=20
management sell well below intrinsic business value - stocks=20
sometimes provide grand-slam home runs.  But we currently find no=20
equities that come close to meeting our tests.  This statement in=20
no way translates into a stock market prediction: we have no idea=20
- and never have had - whether the market is going to go up,=20
down, or sideways in the near- or intermediate term future.

     What we do know, however, is that occasional outbreaks of=20
those two super-contagious diseases, fear and greed, will forever=20
occur in the investment community.  The timing of these epidemics=20
will be unpredictable.  And the market aberrations produced by=20
them will be equally unpredictable, both as to duration and=20
degree.  Therefore, we never try to anticipate the arrival or=20
departure of either disease.  Our goal is more modest: we simply=20
attempt to be fearful when others are greedy and to be greedy=20
only when others are fearful.

     As this is written, little fear is visible in Wall Street. =20
Instead, euphoria prevails - and why not?  What could be more=20
exhilarating than to participate in a bull market in which the=20
rewards to owners of businesses become gloriously uncoupled from=20
the plodding performances of the businesses themselves. =20
Unfortunately, however, stocks can=92t outperform businesses=20
indefinitely.

     Indeed, because of the heavy transaction and investment=20
management costs they bear, stockholders as a whole and over the=20
long term must inevitably underperform the companies they own. =20
If American business, in aggregate, earns about 12% on equity=20
annually, investors must end up earning significantly less.  Bull=20
markets can obscure mathematical laws, but they cannot repeal=20
them.

     The second category of investments open to our insurance=20
companies is long-term bonds.  These are unlikely to be of=20
interest to us except in very special situations, such as the=20
Washington Public Power Supply System #1, #2 and #3 issues,=20
discussed in our 1984 report. (At yearend, we owned WPPSS issues=20
having an amortized cost of $218 million and a market value of=20
$310 million, paying us $31.7 million in annual tax-exempt=20
income.) Our aversion to long-term bonds relates to our fear that=20
we will see much higher rates of inflation within the next=20
decade.  Over time, the behavior of our currency will be=20
determined by the behavior of our legislators.  This relationship=20
poses a continuing threat to currency stability - and a=20
corresponding threat to the owners of long-term bonds.

     We continue to periodically employ money in the arbitrage=20
field.  However, unlike most arbitrageurs, who purchase dozens of=20
securities each year, we purchase only a few.  We restrict=20
ourselves to large deals that have been announced publicly and do=20
not bet on the come.  Therefore, our potential profits are apt to=20
be small; but, with luck, our disappointments will also be few.

     Our yearend portfolio shown below includes one arbitrage=20
commitment, Lear-Siegler.  Our balance sheet also includes a=20
receivable for $145 million, representing the money owed us (and=20
paid a few days later) by Unilever, then in the process of=20
purchasing Chesebrough-Ponds, another of our arbitrage holdings. =20
Arbitrage is an alternative to Treasury Bills as a short-term=20
parking place for money - a choice that combines potentially=20
higher returns with higher risks.  To date, our returns from the=20
funds committed to arbitrage have been many times higher than=20
they would have been had we left those funds in Treasury Bills. =20
Nonetheless, one bad experience could change the scorecard=20
markedly.

     We also, though it takes some straining, currently view=20
medium-term tax-exempt bonds as an alternative to short-term=20
Treasury holdings.  Buying these bonds, we run a risk of=20
significant loss if, as seems probable, we sell many of them well=20
before maturity.  However, we believe this risk is more than=20
counter-balanced first, by the much higher after-tax returns=20
currently realizable from these securities as compared to=20
Treasury Bills and second, by the possibility that sales will=20
produce an overall profit rather than a loss.  Our expectation of=20
a higher total return, after allowing for the possibility of loss=20
and after taking into account all tax effects, is a relatively=20
close call and could well be wrong.  Even if we sell our bonds at=20
a fairly large loss, however, we may end up reaping a higher=20
after-tax return than we would have realized by repeatedly=20
rolling over Treasury Bills.

     In any event, you should know that our expectations for both=20
the stocks and bonds we now hold are exceptionally modest, given=20
current market levels.  Probably the best thing that could happen=20
to us is a market in which we would choose to sell many of our=20
bond holdings at a significant loss in order to re-allocate funds=20
to the far-better equity values then very likely to exist.  The=20
bond losses I am talking about would occur if high interest rates=20
came along; the same rates would probably depress common stocks=20
considerably more than medium-term bonds.

