businesses are worth.

     This attitude may seem old-fashioned in a corporate world in=20
which activity has become the order of the day.  The modern=20
manager refers to his "portfolio" of businesses - meaning that=20
all of them are candidates for "restructuring" whenever such a=20
move is dictated by Wall Street preferences, operating conditions=20
or a new corporate "concept." (Restructuring is defined narrowly,=20
however: it extends only to dumping offending businesses, not to=20
dumping the officers and directors who bought the businesses in=20
the first place.  "Hate the sin but love the sinner" is a=20
theology as popular with the Fortune 500 as it is with the=20
Salvation Army.)

     Investment managers are even more hyperkinetic: their=20
behavior during trading hours makes whirling dervishes appear=20
sedated by comparison.  Indeed, the term "institutional investor"=20
is becoming one of those self-contradictions called an oxymoron,=20
comparable to "jumbo shrimp," "lady mudwrestler" and "inexpensive=20
lawyer."

     Despite the enthusiasm for activity that has swept business=20
and financial America, we will stick with our =91til-death-do-us-
part policy. It=92s the only one with which Charlie and I are=20
comfortable, it produces decent results, and it lets our managers=20
and those of our investees run their businesses free of=20
distractions.

<b>NHP, Inc.
</b>
     Last year we paid $23.7 million for about 50% of NHP, Inc.,=20
a developer, syndicator, owner and manager of multi-family rental=20
housing.  Should all executive stock options that have been=20
authorized be granted and exercised, our equity interest will=20
decline to slightly over 45%.

     NHP, Inc. has a most unusual genealogy.  In 1967, President=20
Johnson appointed a commission of business and civic leaders, led=20
by Edgar Kaiser, to study ways to increase the supply of=20
multifamily housing for low- and moderate-income tenants. =20
Certain members of the commission subsequently formed and=20
promoted two business entities to foster this goal.  Both are now=20
owned by NHP, Inc. and one operates under unusual ground rules:=20
three of its directors must be appointed by the President, with=20
the advice and consent of the Senate, and it is also required by=20
law to submit an annual report to the President.

     Over 260 major corporations, motivated more by the idea of=20
public service than profit, invested $42 million in the two=20
original entities, which promptly began, through partnerships, to=20
develop government-subsidized rental property.  The typical=20
partnership owned a single property and was largely financed by a=20
non-recourse mortgage.  Most of the equity money for each=20
partnership was supplied by a group of limited partners who were=20
primarily attracted by the large tax deductions that went with=20
the investment.  NHP acted as general partner and also purchased=20
a small portion of each partnership=92s equity.

     The Government=92s housing policy has, of course, shifted and=20
NHP has necessarily broadened its activities to include non-
subsidized apartments commanding market-rate rents.  In addition,=20
a subsidiary of NHP builds single-family homes in the Washington,=20
D.C. area, realizing revenues of about $50 million annually.

     NHP now oversees about 500 partnership properties that are=20
located in 40 states, the District of Columbia and Puerto Rico,=20
and that include about 80,000 housing units.  The cost of these=20
properties was more than $2.5 billion and they have been well=20
maintained.  NHP directly manages about 55,000 of the housing=20
units and supervises the management of the rest.  The company=92s=20
revenues from management are about $16 million annually, and=20
growing.

     In addition to the equity interests it purchased upon the=20
formation of each partnership, NHP owns varying residual=20
interests that come into play when properties are disposed of and=20
distributions are made to the limited partners.  The residuals on=20
many of NHP=92s "deep subsidy" properties are unlikely to be of=20
much value.  But residuals on certain other properties could=20
prove quite valuable, particularly if inflation should heat up.

     The tax-oriented syndication of properties to individuals=20
has been halted by the Tax Reform Act of 1986.  In the main, NHP=20
is currently trying to develop equity positions or significant=20
residual interests in non-subsidized rental properties of quality=20
and size (typically 200 to 500 units).  In projects of this kind,=20
NHP usually works with one or more large institutional investors=20
or lenders.  NHP will continue to seek ways to develop low- and=20
moderate-income apartment housing, but will not likely meet=20
success unless government policy changes.

     Besides ourselves, the large shareholders in NHP are=20
Weyerhauser (whose interest is about 25%) and a management group=20
led by Rod Heller, chief executive of NHP.  About 60 major=20
corporations also continue to hold small interests, none larger=20
than 2%.

<b>Taxation
</b>
     The Tax Reform Act of 1986 affects our various businesses in=20
important and divergent ways.  Although we find much to praise in=20
the Act, the net financial effect for Berkshire is negative: our=20
rate of increase in business value is likely to be at least=20
moderately slower under the new law than under the old.  The net=20
effect for our shareholders is even more negative: every dollar=20
of increase in per-share business value, assuming the increase is=20
accompanied by an equivalent dollar gain in the market value of=20
Berkshire stock, will produce 72 cents of after-tax gain for our=20
shareholders rather than the 80 cents produced under the old law. =20
This result, of course, reflects the rise in the maximum tax rate=20
on personal capital gains from 20% to 28%.

     Here are the main tax changes that affect Berkshire:

   <b>o</b> The tax rate on corporate ordinary income is scheduled to=20
decrease from 46% in 1986 to 34% in 1988.  This change obviously=20
affects us positively - and it also has a significant positive=20
effect on two of our three major investees, Capital Cities/ABC=20
and The Washington Post Company.

     I say this knowing that over the years there has been a lot=20
of fuzzy and often partisan commentary about who really pays=20
corporate taxes - businesses or their customers.  The argument,=20
of course, has usually turned around tax increases, not=20
decreases.  Those people resisting increases in corporate rates=20
frequently argue that corporations in reality pay none of the=20
taxes levied on them but, instead, act as a sort of economic=20
pipeline, passing all taxes through to consumers.  According to=20
these advocates, any corporate-tax increase will simply lead to=20
higher prices that, for the corporation, offset the increase. =20
Having taken this position, proponents of the "pipeline" theory=20
must also conclude that a tax decrease for corporations will not=20
help profits but will instead flow through, leading to=20
correspondingly lower prices for consumers.

     Conversely, others argue that corporations not only pay the=20
taxes levied upon them, but absorb them also.  Consumers, this=20
school says, will be unaffected by changes in corporate rates.

     What really happens?  When the corporate rate is cut, do=20
Berkshire, The Washington Post, Cap Cities, etc., themselves soak=20
up the benefits, or do these companies pass the benefits along to=20
their customers in the form of lower prices?  This is an=20
important question for investors and managers, as well as for=20
policymakers.

     Our conclusion is that in some cases the benefits of lower=20
corporate taxes fall exclusively, or almost exclusively, upon the=20
corporation and its shareholders, and that in other cases the=20
benefits are entirely, or almost entirely, passed through to the=20
customer.  What determines the outcome is the strength of the=20
corporation=92s business franchise and whether the profitability of=20
that franchise is regulated.

     For example, when the franchise is strong and after-tax=20
profits are regulated in a relatively precise manner, as is the=20
case with electric utilities, changes in corporate tax rates are=20
largely reflected in prices, not in profits.  When taxes are cut,=20
prices will usually be reduced in short order.  When taxes are=20
increased, prices will rise, though often not as promptly.

     A similar result occurs in a second arena - in the price-
competitive industry, whose companies typically operate with very=20
weak business franchises.  In such industries, the free market=20
"regulates" after-tax profits in a delayed and irregular, but=20
generally effective, manner.  The marketplace, in effect,=20
performs much the same function in dealing with the price-
competitive industry as the Public Utilities Commission does in=20
dealing with electric utilities.  In these industries, therefore,=20
tax changes eventually affect prices more than profits.

     In the case of unregulated businesses blessed with strong=20
franchises, however, it=92s a different story:  the corporation=20
and its shareholders are then the major beneficiaries of tax=20
cuts.  These companies benefit from a tax cut much as the=20
electric company would if it lacked a regulator to force down=20
prices.

     Many of our businesses, both those we own in whole and in=20
part, possess such franchises.  Consequently, reductions in their=20
taxes largely end up in our pockets rather than the pockets of=20
our customers.  While this may be impolitic to state, it is=20
impossible to deny.  If you are tempted to believe otherwise,=20
think for a moment of the most able brain surgeon or lawyer in=20
your area.  Do you really expect the fees of this expert (the=20
local "franchise-holder" in his or her specialty) to be reduced=20
now that the top personal tax rate is being cut from 50% to 28%?

     Your joy at our conclusion that lower rates benefit a number=20
of our operating businesses and investees should be severely=20
tempered, however, by another of our convictions: scheduled 1988=20
tax rates, both individual and corporate, seem totally=20
unrealistic to us.  These rates will very likely bestow a fiscal=20
problem on Washington that will prove incompatible with price=20
stability.  We believe, therefore, that ultimately - within, say,=20
five years - either higher tax rates or higher inflation rates=20
are almost certain to materialize.  And it would not surprise us=20
to see both.

   <b>o</b> Corporate capital gains tax rates have been increased from=20
28% to 34%, effective in 1987.  This change will have an=20
important adverse effect on Berkshire because we expect much of=20
our gain in business value in the future, as in the past, to=20
arise from capital gains.  For example, our three major=20
investment holdings - Cap Cities, GEICO, and Washington Post - at=20
yearend had a market value of over $1.7 billion, close to 75% of=20
the total net worth of Berkshire, and yet they deliver us only=20
about $9 million in annual income.  Instead, all three retain a=20
very high percentage of their earnings, which we expect to=20
eventually deliver us capital gains.

     The new law increases the rate for all gains realized in the=20
future, including the unrealized gains that existed before the=20
law was enacted.  At yearend, we had $1.2 billion of such=20
unrealized gains in our equity investments.  The effect of the new=20
law on our balance sheet will be delayed because a GAAP rule=20
stipulates that the deferred tax liability applicable to=20
unrealized gains should be stated at last year=92s 28% tax rate=20
rather than the current 34% rate.  This rule is expected to change=20
soon.  The moment it does, about $73 million will disappear from=20
our GAAP net worth and be added to the deferred tax account.

   <b>o</b> Dividend and interest income received by our insurance=20
companies will be taxed far more heavily under the new law. =20
First, all corporations will be taxed on 20% of the dividends=20
they receive from other domestic corporations, up from 15% under=20
the old law.  Second, there is a change concerning the residual=20
80% that applies only to property/casualty companies: 15% of that=20
residual will be taxed if the stocks paying the dividends were=20
purchased after August 7, 1986.  A third change, again applying=20
only to property/casualty companies, concerns tax-exempt bonds:=20
interest on bonds purchased by insurers after August 7, 1986 will=20
only be 85% tax-exempt.

     The last two changes are very important.  They mean that our=20
income from the investments we make in future years will be=20
significantly lower than would have been the case under the old=20
law.  My best guess is that these changes alone will eventually=20
reduce the earning power of our insurance operation by at least=20
10% from what we could previously have expected.

   <b>o</b> The new tax law also materially changes the timing of tax=20
payments by property/casualty insurance companies.  One new rule=20
requires us to discount our loss reserves in our tax returns, a=20
change that will decrease deductions and increase taxable income. =20
Another rule, to be phased in over six years, requires us to=20
include 20% of our unearned premium reserve in taxable income.

     Neither rule changes the amount of the annual tax accrual in=20
our reports to you, but each materially accelerates the schedule=20
of payments.  That is, taxes formerly deferred will now be front-
ended, a change that will significantly cut the profitability of=20
our business.  An analogy will suggest the toll: if, upon turning=20
21, you were required to immediately pay tax on all income you=20
were due to receive throughout your life, both your lifetime=20
wealth and your estate would be a small fraction of what they=20
would be if all taxes on your income were payable only when you=20
died.

     Attentive readers may spot an inconsistency in what we say. =20
Earlier, discussing companies in price-competitive industries, we=20
suggested that tax increases or reductions affect these companies=20
relatively little, but instead are largely passed along to their=20
customers.  But now we are saying that tax increases will affect=20
profits of Berkshire=92s property/casualty companies even though=20
they operate in an intensely price-competitive industry.

     The reason this industry is likely to be an exception to our=20
general rule is that not all major insurers will be working with=20
identical tax equations.  Important differences will exist for=20
several reasons: a new alternative minimum tax will materially=20
affect some companies but not others; certain major insurers have=20
huge loss carry-forwards that will largely shield their income=20
from significant taxes for at least a few years; and the results=20
of some large insurers will be folded into the consolidated=20
returns of companies with non-insurance businesses.  These=20
disparate conditions will produce widely varying marginal tax=20
rates in the property/casualty industry.  That will not be the=20
case, however, in most other price-competitive industries, such=20
as aluminum, autos and department stores, in which the major=20
players will generally contend with similar tax equations.

     The absence of a common tax calculus for property/casualty=20
companies means that the increased taxes falling on the industry=20
will probably not be passed along to customers to the degree that=20
they would in a typical price-competitive industry.  Insurers, in=20
other words, will themselves bear much of the new tax burdens.

   <b>o</b> A partial offset to these burdens is a "fresh start"=20
adjustment that occurred on January 1, 1987 when our December 31,=20
1986 loss reserve figures were converted for tax purposes to the=20
newly-required discounted basis. (In our reports to you, however,=20
reserves will remain on exactly the same basis as in the past -=20
undiscounted except in special cases such as structured=20
settlements.) The net effect of the "fresh start" is to give us a=20
double deduction: we will get a tax deduction in 1987 and future=20
years for a portion of our-incurred-but-unpaid insurance losses=20
that have already been fully deducted as costs in 1986 and=20
earlier years.

     The increase in net worth that is produced by this change is=20
not yet reflected in our financial statements.  Rather, under=20
present GAAP rules (which may be changed), the benefit will flow=20
into the earnings statement and, consequently, into net worth=20
over the next few years by way of reduced tax charges.  We expect=20
the total benefit from the fresh-start adjustment to be in the=20
$30 - $40 million range.  It should be noted, however, that this=20
is a one-time benefit, whereas the negative impact of the other=20
insurance-related tax changes is not only ongoing but, in=20
important respects, will become more severe as time passes.

   <b>o</b> The General Utilities Doctrine was repealed by the new tax=20
law. This means that in 1987 and thereafter there will be a=20
double tax on corporate liquidations, one at the corporate level=20
and another at the shareholder level.  In the past, the tax at=20
the corporate level could be avoided, If Berkshire, for example,=20
were to be liquidated - which it most certainly won=92t be -=20
shareholders would, under the new law, receive far less from the=20
sales of our properties than they would have if the properties=20
had been sold in the past, assuming identical prices in each=20
sale.  Though this outcome is theoretical in our case, the change=20
in the law will very materially affect many companies. =20
Therefore, it also affects our evaluations of prospective=20
investments.  Take, for example, producing oil and gas=20
businesses, selected media companies, real estate companies, etc.=20
that might wish to sell out.  The values that their shareholders=20
can realize are likely to be significantly reduced simply because=20
the General Utilities Doctrine has been repealed - though the=20
companies=92 operating economics will not have changed adversely at=20
all.  My impression is that this important change in the law has=20
not yet been fully comprehended by either investors or managers.

     This section of our report has been longer and more=20
complicated than I would have liked.  But the changes in the law=20
are many and important, particularly for property/casualty=20
insurers.  As I have noted, the new law will hurt Berkshire=92s=20
results, but the negative impact is impossible to quantify with=20
any precision.

<b>Miscellaneous
</b>
     <font size=3D"-2">We bought a corporate jet last year.</font>  What yo=
u have heard about such=20
planes is true: they are very expensive and a luxury in=20
situations like ours where little travel to out-of-the-way places=20
is required.  And planes not only cost a lot to operate, they cost=20
a lot just to look at.  Pre-tax, cost of capital plus depreciation=20
on a new $15 million plane probably runs $3 million annually.  On=20
our own plane, bought for $850,000 used, such costs run close to=20
$200,000 annually.

     Cognizant of such figures, your Chairman, unfortunately, has=20
in the past made a number of rather intemperate remarks about=20
corporate jets.  Accordingly, prior to our purchase, I was forced=20
into my Galileo mode.  I promptly experienced the necessary=20
"counter-revelation" and travel is now considerably easier - and=20
considerably costlier - than in the past.  Whether Berkshire will=20
get its money=92s worth from the plane is an open question, but I=20
will work at achieving some business triumph that I can (no=20
matter how dubiously) attribute to it.  I=92m afraid Ben Franklin=20
had my number.  Said he: "So convenient a thing it is to be a=20
reasonable creature, since it enables one to find or make a=20
reason for everything one has a mind to do."

     About 97% of all eligible shares participated in Berkshire=92s=20
1986 shareholder-designated contributions program.  Contributions=20
made through the program were $4 million, and 1,934 charities=20
were recipients.

     We urge new shareholders to read the description of our=20
shareholder-designated contributions program that appears on=20
pages 58 and 59.  If you wish to participate in future programs,=20
we strongly urge that you immediately make sure your shares are=20
registered in the name of the actual owner, not in "street" name=20
or nominee name.  Shares not so registered on September 30, 1987=20
will be ineligible for the 1987 program.

                           *  *  *

     Last year almost 450 people attended our shareholders=92=20
meeting, up from about 250 the year before (and from about a=20
dozen ten years ago).  I hope you can join us on May 19th in=20
Omaha.  Charlie and I like to answer owner-related questions=20
and I can promise you that our shareholders will pose many good=20
ones.  Finishing up the questions may take quite a while - we=20
had about 65 last year so you should feel free to leave once=20
your own have been answered.

     Last year, after the meeting, one shareholder from New=20
Jersey and another from New York went to the Furniture Mart,=20
where each purchased a $5,000 Oriental rug from Mrs. B. (To be=20
precise, they purchased rugs that might cost $10,000 elsewhere=20
for which they were charged about $5,000.) Mrs. B was pleased -=20
but not satisfied - and she will be looking for you at the store=20
after this year=92s meeting.  Unless our shareholders top last=20
year=92s record, I=92ll be in trouble.  So do me (and yourself) a=20
favor, and go see her.



                                         Warren E. Buffett
February 27, 1987                        Chairman of the Board</pre>
